NAME
    jbod - pool several disks into one mount (opt-in)

SYNOPSIS
    jbod
    jbod list
    jbod create <mountpoint> <disk> [disk...]
    jbod destroy <mountpoint>

DESCRIPTION
    JBOD ("Just a Bunch Of Disks") presents several filesystem components as a
    SINGLE mount point. Capacity is the sum of the members; a read searches
    every member; a write lands on whichever member already holds the file, or
    else the member with the most free space. It is a union mount, not striping
    or parity — there is no per-block math (slow on OC), and losing one member
    loses only THAT member's files, leaving the rest of the pool readable. That
    makes JBOD strictly safer than RAID-0 for OpenComputers.

    securefs still mediates every operation on the mount point, so per-user
    ACLs apply to the pool exactly as they would to a single disk.

    OPT-IN: JBOD does not ship active. It loads only when /etc/boot.cfg has
    advanced.jbod = true (off in every profile, including 'full'). Enable it
    with the Boot Settings editor (press DEL during boot) or:

        bootsettings jbod on        # then reboot

    Until enabled, the kernel module is not loaded and the 'jbod' command is
    hidden from install-aware help.

SUBCOMMANDS
    (none) / list    Show configured pools and how many members are present.
    create           Build a pool from the listed disks (address prefix or
                     label, as in 'mount'/'lsdev'), mount it, and persist it to
                     /etc/jbod.cfg so it re-mounts on the next boot. (admin)
    destroy          Unmount the pool and remove it from /etc/jbod.cfg. (admin)
                     The member disks and their files are left untouched.

EXAMPLES
    bootsettings jbod on            # enable, then reboot
    jbod create /mnt/pool 1a2b 3c4d # pool two disks by address prefix
    jbod list
    jbod destroy /mnt/pool

FILES
    /etc/jbod.cfg    Pool table: { pools = { { mount=, members={addr,...} } } }

NOTES
    A pool whose every member is missing at boot is left unmounted (logged), so
    a pulled disk can never wedge boot. Cross-member directory renames are
    refused; cross-member file renames copy-then-delete.

SEE ALSO
    mount, umount, df, bootsettings, MANUAL.md (Chapter 5)
